<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      Two indexes for queries added by the Pressure work (042-044). Both were
      measured, not guessed - see the numbers in each changeset below.

      Neither changes a result. An index cannot alter what a query returns, so
      there is no Rule 0 exposure here and no before/after trade comparison is
      required. What they change is whether a full-year replay takes four hours
      or a few minutes.
    -->

    <!--
      chain-volume aggregate: (stock_code, exchange_code, expiry_date, datetime)

      MEASURED PROBLEM
      ================
      HistoricalOptionCandleRepository.sumVolumeByBar - the front-weekly chain
      volume behind the Pressure VWAP proxy - showed up as the hottest statement
      of the whole replay in performance_schema:

          calls 764 | avg 414 ms | avg rows examined 11,030 | total 316 s

      WHY THE EXISTING INDEX CANNOT SERVE IT
      ======================================
      uk_historical_option_series_time is
          (stock_code, exchange_code, expiry_date, strike_price, option_right, datetime)

      The query filters stock_code + exchange_code + expiry_date, ranges on
      datetime, and GROUPs BY datetime. It deliberately does NOT constrain
      strike_price or option_right, because summing across the whole chain is
      the entire point of it.

      Those two columns sit at positions 4 and 5, *ahead of* datetime at 6. A
      composite B-tree can only be used left-to-right up to the first column
      that is not an equality match, so MySQL gets three columns of prefix and
      then has to scan every row of that expiry - all strikes, both rights,
      every date - and filter datetime afterwards. 11,030 rows examined per call
      is exactly that scan.

      This index puts datetime immediately after expiry_date, so the range is
      seekable and the GROUP BY streams in index order with no sort.

      WHY NOT REORDER THE UNIQUE KEY INSTEAD
      ======================================
      It is a UNIQUE constraint and it is what makes the natural key natural -
      (series, time) identifies a candle. Reordering it would let two rows with
      the same strike and right at the same timestamp coexist, which is the one
      thing that key exists to prevent. A second, non-unique index is the
      cheaper and safer answer.
    -->
    <changeSet id="045_index_option_chain_volume_by_bar" author="moneymaker">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <indexExists tableName="historical_option_candles"
                             indexName="idx_hoc_chain_volume_by_bar"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <createIndex tableName="historical_option_candles"
                     indexName="idx_hoc_chain_volume_by_bar">
            <column name="stock_code"/>
            <column name="exchange_code"/>
            <column name="expiry_date"/>
            <column name="datetime"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      book cap: (strategy_id, status, trade_config_id)

      MEASURED PROBLEM
      ================
      TradeOrderRepository.countOpenInBook - the one-position-per-book cap added
      in 042 - runs on every candidate entry signal, roughly 1,000 times per
      replayed day. EXPLAIN reports:

          type: ALL   key: NULL   (i.e. a full scan of trade_order)

      The only usable index, idx_trade_order_open_lookup, leads with
      trade_config_id; this query does not filter on it (that is the whole point
      - it counts ACROSS the configs in a book) so the index cannot be used.

      WHY IT MATTERS EVEN THOUGH IT IS CURRENTLY CHEAP
      ================================================
      At the few hundred rows a run starts with, a full scan costs nothing -
      which is exactly why this would have gone unnoticed. But trade_order grows
      to several thousand rows over a full year while the call count per day
      stays flat, so the cost is quadratic in the length of the run: the last
      day of a replay pays several times what the first day paid, and the
      slowdown looks like "the machine got busy" rather than like a missing
      index.

      COLUMN ORDER
      ============
      strategy_id and status are both equality predicates and are the selective
      pair; trade_config_id trails so the join back to trade_config can be
      satisfied from the index rather than the row.
    -->
    <changeSet id="045_index_trade_order_book_cap" author="moneymaker">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <indexExists tableName="trade_order" indexName="idx_trade_order_book_cap"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <createIndex tableName="trade_order" indexName="idx_trade_order_book_cap">
            <column name="strategy_id"/>
            <column name="status"/>
            <column name="trade_config_id"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
